%N A398217 Decimal expansion of the large face angle, in radians, of a canonical (dual-uniform) octagonal trapezohedron.
%C A398217 The octagonal trapezohedron is the dual polyhedron of the octagonal antiprism.
%C A398217 Its faces are kites with one acute angle (A398216) and three obtuse angles (this constant).
%H A398217 Paolo Xausa, <a href="/A398217/b398217.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%H A398217 David I. McCooey, <a href="https://dmccooey.com/polyhedra/OctagonalTrapezohedron.html">Octagonal Trapezohedron</a>.
%H A398217 Polytope Wiki, <a href="https://polytope.miraheze.org/wiki/Octagonal_antitegum">Octagonal antitegum</a>.
%H A398217 Eric Weisstein's World of Mathematics, <a href="https://mathworld.wolfram.com/Trapezohedron.html">Trapezohedron</a>.
%H A398217 <a href="/index/Tra#transcendental">Index entries for transcendental numbers</a>.
%F A398217 Equals arccos(1/2 - cos(Pi/8)) = arccos(1/2 - A144981).
%F A398217 Equals arccos(c), where c = -0.4238795... is the smallest root of 16*x^4 - 32*x^3 + 8*x^2 + 8*x - 1.
%e A398217 2.00852075040673308582398867993975570886156876879775893...
%t A398217 First[RealDigits[ArcCos[1/2 - Cos[Pi/8]], 10, 100]]
%o A398217 (PARI) acos(1/2-cos(Pi/8)) \\ _Charles R Greathouse IV_, Aug 03 2026
